{
  "id": "Compound::DB00284 - Gene::278",
  "verbalization": "Acarbose exerts its pharmacological effects by binding to Amy1a and Amy1b, two isoforms of the enzyme amyloplast, which are involved in the catalytic activity of alpha-amylase. This binding is a critical step in the mechanism of action of Acarbose, as it inhibits the enzymatic activity of alpha-amylase. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Acarbose interacts with Amy1a and Amy1b, both of which are involved in the same enzymatic process, leading to the inhibition of alpha-amylase activity. The shared involvement of these isoforms in the same enzymatic activity suggests a functional relationship between Acarbose and Amy1c, as both Amy1a and Amy1b are part of the same pathway that is ultimately participated by Amy1c. The integration of these findings highlights a mechanism where Acarbose acts as a competitive inhibitor of alpha-amylase, targeting the enzyme's active site through interaction with its regulatory subunits, thereby modulating the overall enzymatic activity.",
